Z||000006||Monkeyman||06-17-2000||09:27 AM||||My fault.  I don't really see the point in a 120 mph speedo, though.  (Yes, I have one in my car, but not by choice.)  I'd rather have the '86 SE gauges (backlit, "GT" style tach, 85mph speedo, MUCH cheaper).  I can see going slightly faster than 85 if the conditions were right, but I think 120 is just a little dangerous.  Just my opinion, not the opinion of the management.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||06-17-2000||12:07 PM||||It's also easier to get out of a ticket with an 85 MPH speedo.  When the cop says you were going 120, you say, "Well, Mr. Police officer, my speedometer told me I was going 85.  So if you don't mind, I'll be on my way.  You'd get a ticket anyway, and probably a smack in the face as well.  Anyway, I like the power window and door locks on my 86 GT.  Neither of them work the best, though.  Actually nothing powered works completely.  The locks only work on the left side, the window on the right gets stuck on the power lock motor and won't roll up past a certain point, and the mirrors on the right go up and down, but don't work in and out.  Sometimes, manual is the key.||||reg||6
Z||000008||TOM||06-17-2000||12:55 PM||||MONKEY. if you did not want the 120 then why did you buy it. I installed the 120 because my car now has all the opinions. and the speedo was $8. You saw it was not by choice. which says it came with the car then you say it was a plug and play item in your car which says you installed it.<BR>fropuff. I understand that power options get old and need attention. but your car is between 12 and 15 years old. everything needs some attention. And personnally I like the options. I like being able to unlock the doors with the remote. I like being able to open the other window without leaning across the car. But that just me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||06-17-2000||05:17 PM||||Tom--I bought a set of "86 SE gauges for the backlight and GT tach (installed aux gauges).  While I was trying to reinstall the standard gauge cluster, I messed up some of the metal-on-plastic connections on the back.  I was going out of town in a few days and needed some gauges, so I scrambled trying to find something that would work.  (I was told that mine couldn't be fixed.)  I finally got a hold of Bob Steiger and he happened to have a set of '87 GT gauges he could send right out.  If I could have found the (much) cheaper SE gauges, I would have bought those.  Now the tach on my "new" gauges reads about 1500rpms higher than it should.  I'll try running another ground to see if that makes a difference.  If not, it's another call to Bob.   ?????||||reg||1
